===Glyph origin===
{{Han etym}}

Originally {{Han compound|魚|口|ls=ic|t1=fish|t2=mouth|nocap=y}} — fish entering the mouth; hence "delicious" → "excellent". In oracle bone and bronze inscriptions, often written simply as {{zh-l|*魚}}; {{zh-l|*口}} was added to differentiate this word from "fish". The {{zh-l|*口}} component later merged with the fish tail to resemble {{zh-l|*白}}, leading to the ''{{w|Shuowen}}'' misanalysis {{lang|zh|从白，鮺省聲}}.

===Etymology 1===
The original meaning is "excellent; fine", attested in Shang oracle bone harvest divinations (alongside {{zh-l|*吉}}) and Western Zhou bronze blessing formulae ({{zh-l|*純魯}}, {{zh-l|*魯休}}, {{zh-l|*大魯福}}). Confirmed by ''{{w|Records of the Grand Historian}}'', where the same event appears as {{lang|zh|'''魯'''天子之命}} ("Basic Annals of Zhou") and {{lang|zh|'''嘉'''天子命}} ("Hereditary House of Lu").

Possibly related to {{och-l|路|large|tr=*C.rˤah}}, {{och-l|旅|numerous|tr=*raʔ}}.

