===Etymology 1===
{{ja-kanjitab|くじら|yomi=k}}
{{IPAchar|/kudira/}} → {{IPAchar|/kud͡ʑira/}}

From {{inh|ja|ojp|-}}.  Appears in the ''{{w|Kojiki}}'' of 712 CE with the {{w|man'yōgana}} spelling {{m|ja||久治良|tr=kudira}}. Hypothesized as ultimately from reconstructed {{inh|ja|jpx-pro|*kuntira}}.

Ultimate derivation unclear. Theories include an alteration from {{ja-l|口}}{{ja-l|広}} (''kuchibiro'') in reference to the wide mouth;<ref>'''1891''', 言海 (''Genkai'', “Sea of Words”) (in Japanese), lead editor {{w|Ōtsuki Fumihiko}}.  Entry available online [http://www.babelbible.net/genkai/genkai.cgi?mode=right&imode=0&noexpand=0&page=281 here].</ref> an alteration from {{ja-l|口}}{{ja-l|開く}} (''kuchibiraku'') in reference to the gaping mouth; and an alteration from {{ja-l|黒白}} (''kuroshiro'') in reference to the black-and-white coloration of certain [[cetacean]]s.<ref>'''2007-2014''', 日本辞典 (''Nihon Jiten'', “Japan Dictionary”), 生物語源辞典 (''Seibutsu Gogen Jiten'', “Living Organism Etymology Dictionary”) section (in Japanese).  Entry available online [http://www.nihonjiten.com/data/45904.html here].</ref>

However, the oldest sources clearly spell the term as ''kudira''.
* This rules out {{m|ja|口|tr=kuti → kuchi||mouth}} as an [[etymon]], as the oldest compounds using this term use the bound form ''kutu'', not ''kuti'', and there is no support for a ''kutu'' → ''kudi'' shift.
* This also rules out {{m|ja|白|tr=siro, sira → shiro, shira||white}} as an [[etymon]].  Although [[rendaku]] (voicing in compounds) would give modern ''jira'' as a reading for {{m|ja|白}}, the ancient reading of ''sira'' would voice to something closer to ''zira'', and there is no support for a ''zira'' → ''dira'' shift.

Also compare {{cog|ko|고래}} "whale."

